            <div xml:id="d1365">

                <pb facs="1072" n="956" sameAs="frus:frus1912/pg956"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 412.11/59.</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The American Ambassador to
                        the Secretary of State</hi>.</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 1266.]</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">American Embassy</hi>, <lb/>
                            <hi rendition="#italic">Mexico</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1912-02-19">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">February 19, 1912</hi></date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: Referring to the matter of the American
                    claims growing out of the recent revolution I have the honor to inform the
                    Department that on February 15th I informally requested Mr. Calero, Mexican
                    Minister for Foreign Affairs, to inform me what action had been taken therein.
                    Mr. Calero stated that instructions had already been sent to the Mexican Consul
                    at El Paso to institute an investigation into the matter of the claims growing
                    out of the El Paso-Ciudad Juárez incident. He said that he had no confidence in
                    the Mexican Consul at Douglas and was making a change in the consular
                    representation there in order that the Mexican Government might have the benefit
                    of an intelligent and thorough investigation into the facts. Mr. Calero thought
                    that this contemplated change might, be brought about in two or three days. Mr.
                    Calero further said that he had referred the Alamo cases to the Minister of
                    Justice, who is understood to be arranging the method of judicial
                    investigation.</p>
                <p>I have [etc.]</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Henry Lane Wilson.</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
